    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "After upgrading from 2.7.5 to 3.0.0, any page using {% render_table %} raises:\n\ndjango.template.exceptions.TemplateSyntaxError: Could not parse the remainder:\n'=column.order_by_alias.next' from 'table.prefixed_order_by_field=column.order_by_alias.next'\nRoot cause:\n\nIn 3.0.0 the {% querystring %} tag was rewritten as a @simple_tag. Django's standard parse_bits() parser (used by @simple_tag) requires keyword argument names to be plain identifiers — dots are not allowed.\n\nHowever, the bundled django_tables2/table.html template still uses the old syntax:\n\n{% querystring table.prefixed_order_by_field=column.order_by_alias.next %}\ntable.prefixed_order_by_field contains a dot, so parse_bits() rejects it at template compile time. The package's own template is incompatible with the package's own tag in this release.\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n\nInstall django-tables2==3.0.0\nUse {% render_table table %} with the default django_tables2/table.html template\nAny request to that page raises TemplateSyntaxError\nExpected behaviour: The bundled templates work with the bundled template tags.\nWorkaround: Pin to django-tables2==2.7.5",
